window.REFINED={"Genesis": {"1": {"1": {"m": "From time immemorial man has been held spellbound by the question of the beginning — the beginning of man, and of the world. And here, in the very first line of God's word, we are given the answer.\n\nIn the beginning, God.\n\nThat single phrase answers what men of all ages have asked: what was there at the very beginning, and who was there? I have heard the argument that in the beginning there was nothing, and out of that nothing came something. But Scripture informs us otherwise — God was in the beginning.\n\nInterestingly, this verse is less concerned with *what* was there at the beginning than with *what was happening*: there was a Being doing the creating, who by implication already existed before the heavens and the earth were made.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "From time immemorial man has been held spellbound by the question of the beginning. The beginning of man and the world. Now here in the word of God, we have the answer- what happened at the very beginning.\n\nSo that answers the question men from all ages have asked... In the beginning was God.\n\n I have heard the arguments being made that in the beginning was nothing and out of that nothing came something. But here we are informed as to what was in the beginning. God was in the beginning.\n\nInterestingly this verse was not addressing so much what was there in the beginning as much as what was happening in the beginning.\n\nonly\n\nThis therefore lets us in on the fact that there was a being doing the creation and by implications existed before the creation of the heavens and the earth."], ["Oct 2023", "From time immemorial man has been held spellbound by the question of the beginning... In the beginning God. So that answers the question men of all ages have asked... This therefore lets us in on the fact that there was a being doing the creation and by implications existed before the creation of the heaven and the earth."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts; removed the stray word “only”; tightened flow into paragraphs. No ideas changed.", "e": ""}, "2": {"m": "\"And the earth became without form…\" Dake's commentary — which I use personally — explains that the word \"was\" actually carries the sense of \"became.\" So I checked the Strong's definition of the Hebrew, and found a remarkable range: to be, to become, to come to pass, to exist, to happen, to come into being, to arise, to appear. A tall order of meanings — but the point stands: \"the earth came to be without form.\"\n\nWe see God create the heavens and the earth, and then the earth — part of His own creation — becomes formless and void. What happened? What caused such waste and destruction?\n\nSome theorize this is the flood of Lucifer and a pre-adamite world. I haven't done much research there myself, so I'll leave it hanging until I'm personally convinced — but it's worth mentioning.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "And the earth became without form...Dake's commentary bible... explained that the word \"was\" actually means to became. I thus checked the strong definition of the word 'was' in Hebrew and here is what I got... [long Strong's list] ... the point is this. \"the earth came to be without form\"... Some théorize that, that is the flood of Lucifer and the pré adamite world. I have not personally done much research into that so I'll will leave that hanging till I'm personally convinced..."], ["Oct 2023", "And the earth became without Form...Dakes commentary bible which is what I use explained that the word was actually mean to become... [Strong's list] ... What caused such waste and destruction..??? Some théorize that is the flood of Lucifer and the pré adamite world..."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts; condensed the long Strong's word-list to its sense; kept your conclusion and caution verbatim in meaning.", "e": ""}, "3": {"m": "God called light to be, and it was so. Where did the light come from, and how was it formed? Scripture doesn't tell us — but one thing we notice is that the light came *after* God spoke. The text emphasizes this: \"God said… and there was light,\" stressing that the light appeared only after it was spoken forth.\n\nThis is so important, because all through the rest of the Bible we see this pattern set in stone: God executes His will by speaking forth words. As Amos puts it — \"Surely the Lord God will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets… the Lord God hath spoken, who can but prophesy?\" (Amos 3:7-8). This theme of God requiring His servants to prophesy and speak forth recurs again and again throughout Scripture.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "Where did the light come from and how was it formed?... the light came after God spoke... This is so important because all through the rest of the Bible we see this pattern set in stone, God executes his will through speaking forth of words.\n\nThis is so important because all through the rest of the Bible we see this pattern set in stone, God executes his will through speaking forth of words.\n\nAmos 3:7-8..."], ["Oct 2023", "God called light to be and it was so. Where did the light come from...? ... To emphasis this we are told \"God said... and there was light\"... we see God execute his will through speaking forth of words. Amos 3:7-8..."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts; removed a duplicated sentence; folded the Amos quote in cleanly.", "e": ""}, "4": {"m": "After the Spirit hovers over the waters, God speaks forth light, and then goes to work to separate the light from the darkness. There seems to be a pattern here — speaking forth *before* actually making what was spoken into matter (whether matter that did not exist, or matter made from the words themselves). I'll watch to see whether this holds through the rest of Scripture, or was only applied here.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "After the spirit hovers on the waters, God speaks forth light and then God goes to work to separate light from darkness. I'll see if this forms a pattern in the rest of the creation narrative or was only applied here. That is the pattern of speaking forth before actually going ahead to make what was spoken from word matter..."], ["Oct 2023", "After the spirits hovers on the waters, God speaks forth light and then God goes to work to separate light from darkness. I'll see if this forms a pattern in the rest of scripture or was only applied here."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts.", "e": "Your understanding grew: in Feb 2023 you wondered if the pattern held only in <b>the creation narrative</b>; by Oct 2023 you widened it to <b>the rest of Scripture</b>. The refined version keeps your later, broader view."}, "5": {"m": "God again begins to name everything He created.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "God again begins to name everything he created"]], "c": "Single note; only spacing tidied.", "e": ""}, "6": {"m": "We see a repetition of the same format: God calls forth what He wants, then He goes to work on it and makes it, and then He gives the finished product a name. Let's call this the **law of the creative process**.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "We see a repetition of the same format. God calls forth what he wants, then he goes ahead to work on it and make it...Then gives the finished product a name. Let's call this the\nlaw\nof the creative process."], ["Oct 2023", "V\n\nWe see a repetition of the same formate. That is God calls forth what he wants, then he goes ahead to work on it and makes it. He then gives the finished product a name. Let's call this the law of creative process."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts; removed stray “V” and the broken line-break around “law of the creative process”; fixed “formate” → “format.”", "e": ""}, "9": {"m": "Let's see if the law of the creative process is followed here:\n\n1. God calls forth — commands — what He wants to see.\n2. The thing appears, and God goes ahead to work out and create what He spoke forth.\n3. He names the final product.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "Let's see if the law of creative process is followed here...\n1. God calls forth or commands what he wants to see\n2. The things appear and God goes ahead to work out and create what he spoke forth\n3. He names the final product"], ["Oct 2023", "Let's see if the law of creative process is followed here...1. God call forth or commands what he wants to see2. The things appear...3. He names the final product."]], "c": "Merged 2 drafts; restored the numbered list that had collapsed into one line in the later draft.", "e": ""}, "13": {"m": "The evening and the morning. Is it not interesting that evening is mentioned *before* morning, and the whole called a day? This same order is followed in the Jewish Sabbath, where a day runs from sunset one evening to sunset the next.", "d": [["Feb 2023", "The evening and morning. Is it not interesting that evening is mentioned before morning and called day. This order is followed in the Jewish Sabbath where a day is from sunset in the evening to sunset the following evening."], ["Oct 2023", "The evening and morning. Is it not interesting that evening is mentioned before morning"]], "c": "Kept the fuller Feb 2023 note (the Oct draft trailed off mid-thought).", "e": ""}, "28": {"m": "**Be fruitful:** be productive — create and produce something of worth out of the resources available to you. The blessing is like a seed harnessing soil and sunlight to grow into a giant tree and then bear fruit: something creative and beneficial, drawn from the resources around you and your own internal gifting and abilities. A mango seed produces a fruit unique from an orange, according to its own inherent nature.\n\n**Multiply:** akin to fruitfulness, but here you duplicate yourself — the fruit carries within it the seed, the multiplier. We as men are meant to multiply ourselves.\n\n**Replenish:** to fill a vacant space. God is saying: don't just multiply — multiply until you fill the entire container, the earth. Multiply without limit, restraint, or complacency. The limit is only when the earth is full.\n\n**And subdue it:** so you are to fill the whole earth — but more than that, be the dominant force. This implies opposing forces to your dominance. You can multiply and replenish and yet still be subdued; filling the earth doesn't automatically make you the dominant force. (In the slave-trade and colonisation era, those who subdued the colonies were not necessarily the majority — yet they subdued all else.) So man is to subdue.\n\nIt's worth noting that man was not given dominion over other men, or over his own offspring — at least, that is not stated before the fall.\n\nAnd in all of history, only one Man has fully accomplished this mandate — Jesus Christ: absolute mastery over sea, land, and air. He walked on water, stilled the tempestuous wind, healed the sick, restored eyes, and cast out demons and every intruder in the earth realm.", "d": [["Original study note", "Be Fruitful: be productive... Multiply... Replenish... And Subdue it... For instance in the slave trade and colonisation era... man was not to have dominion over other men; over his offspring. At least that was not stated before the fall."], ["Feb 2023", "In all of history we see only one man who was able to successfully accomplish this mandate... ie the man Jesus Christ... He walked on water stopped the tempestuous wind, healed the sick, repaired eyes. Caste out demons and other trespassers..."], ["Oct 2023", "In all of history we see only one man... Jesus christ... Caste out demons and other trespassers and intruders"]], "c": "Merged 3 notes (your detailed word-study + two short drafts on Christ) into one flowing reflection; “Caste” → “cast.”", "e": ""}}, "2": {"4": {"m": "Strange. So what exactly happened in Chapter 1? Was it all a charade? God calls forth creation — sky, earth, sea — and even blesses them. Yet here in Chapter 2 we look at the earth and there is nothing visible, because the earth was still waiting for rain, which is what causes things to grow. And there was no man yet — that, too, is given as a reason for the absence of visible growth.\n\nThis brings us to a great principle: God is a God of order, wisdom and process. Within a simple seed is a whole tree that can nest birds and feed animals — all that potential hidden in one seed. It is the same here: process and order, not random calling-forth.\n\nMan, for whom all this was created, needed to be present before the things created matured to fullness — the caretaker had to be there first. So the work God had finished and rested from had not yet materialised, for two reasons: there was not yet rain to trigger growth, and the man to till the ground was not yet present. So God formed a fully-grown man, planted him in the garden, caused the trees to grow and bear fruit for him, and from there left everything to its natural course.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Strange hmm 🤔. So what exactly happened in Chapter 1?... the earth was waiting for rain... there was no man... God is a God of order wisdom and processes. Within a simple seed is a wonderful tree... Two reasons why the work which the lord had completed and rested from had not yet materialised 1. There was not yet rain... 2. The man... was not yet present to till the earth. So God formed a fully grown man and planted him in the garden..."]], "c": "Light polish — paragraphs and flow; meaning unchanged.", "e": ""}, "8": {"m": "A perfect chicken-and-egg scenario: which did God create first? Here we see He created both. He laid the foundation of the earth — plants, animals, every productive living thing — and hid it in the seed, awaiting rain. Then He goes ahead and plants a complete, fully-grown garden, and a fully-formed man to tend it.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "A perfect chicken 🐥 and egg 🥚 scenario. Which one did God create first? Here we see God created both. He laid the foundation for the earth... hid it in the seed awaiting rain. Then he goes ahead and plants a complete fully grown garden and a fully formed man to tend the garden"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "15": {"m": "To dress it — understood. But to *keep* it? Keep it from whom, and from what?\n\nFrom the very onset of creation there has been this subtle suggestion of an enemy — one placed in the earth who needed to be kept out. Consider that in Genesis 1:28 man was commanded to *subdue* the earth. Why subdue, if there were no rebellion, or at least the possibility of it?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "To dress? Well understood but to keep it? Keep it from who and what. From the unset of creation, there has been this subtle suggestion of an enemy... for instance in Genesis 1:28 man was commanded to subdue the earth. Why subdue if there is no rebellion or possibility of it?\n28 And God blessed them... and subdue it: and have dominion..."]], "c": "Polish; kept your Genesis 1:28 cross-reference, trimmed the pasted full verse text.", "e": ""}, "17": {"m": "The nature of the tree could not be imparted merely by speaking about it — even though it is the tree of *knowledge*. Quite ironic. How else, then, was the knowledge to be imparted? By eating it. It was almost like a pen-drive carrying malware, transmitted through the hardware.\n\nNotice that God could *mention* good and evil, yet Adam and Eve still had no knowledge of the distinction. The desire for evil was not in them; they knew only good — they were good by nature. But the fruit would introduce a knowledge into them.\n\nDoes that mean they didn't know good either? I don't think so. They knew good — but because it was all they knew, there was nothing to compare it to, nothing by which to say \"this is good and that is evil.\" You can only distinguish the two if you know both.\n\nFor example: many of us who grew up in poor communities in Africa did not know we were poor. We simply lived; things were hard, but we were happy — until we moved out and grew up and realised we had been poor. In the same way, the tree made the knowledge of evil part of us — flesh, soul, even spirit. We were contaminated. That is why, the moment they ate, they saw they were naked, and felt its shame. (And God's response to their hiding was a strange question — see the notes on Genesis 3:10-11.)", "d": [["Oct 2023", "The nature of the tree could not be imparted by communicating it though is a tree of knowledge... It was almost like a pen drive containing malware... They knew only good. They were good by nature... You can only distinguish the two if you know both... for most of us in Africa who grew up in a poor community, we did not know we were poor... The tree made knowledge of evil part of us... Move to chapter three verses 10 to 11 notes."]], "c": "Polish and paragraphing; turned your 'move to ch3:10-11' aside into a clean cross-reference.", "e": ""}, "18": {"m": "What God had seen as good — indeed *very* good — was the created order and the making of man. Now He sees the man alone and says \"not good.\" So He goes to work to bring good to him: the various animal forms and shapes, which Adam named, yet none was comparable to him. Then God puts the man to sleep, forms another creature, and brings her to him — and Adam himself confirms that *this* one is comparable, \"bone of his bone and flesh of his flesh.\" He names her woman. This was indeed \"very good.\"", "d": [["Oct 2023", "What God saw that was good, indeed very good was the created things... And now he sees the man alone and says \"not good\"... none was comparable to him. He then puts man to sleep and forms another creature... He gives her the name woman. This was indeed \"very good\""]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "19": {"m": "Adam was highly intelligent — remarkably so. His language was already fully formed, and his understanding so vast that he could create and coin language itself. Thus he named the animals, inventing new, non-existent words as he named them.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Adam was so highly intelligent and super smart. His language was already fully formed... Thus he named the animals, creating new non-existent language as he named them."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "20": {"m": "So a help was found for Adam, but none truly *meet* — comparable — for him. (Note that the animals were not eating one another, nor was man eating them; their purpose was variety, and a support system for man.) Since none was like him, God went back to work, sought out a help meet, and when He had finished, brought her to Adam just as He had the other creatures. And there Adam saw that this one was bone of his and flesh of his, unlike the rest — and he named her Woman.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "So there was found help for Adam but not comparable/meet for him. Note that animals were not eating each other... the purpose of other creatures was for variety and as a support system for man... none was like him. So God went back to work... And he gave her the name Woman"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "21": {"m": "And hence the popular expression said of a man who finds a wife: *you have found your missing rib.*", "d": [["Oct 2023", "And hence the popular expression said of a man who finds a wife... You have found your missing rib"]], "c": "No change beyond spacing.", "e": ""}, "25": {"m": "Worth noting — because it ties directly into the eating of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil in chapter three.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "This point is worth noting because it ties in with the eating of the tree of good and evil in chapter three."]], "c": "No change beyond spacing.", "e": ""}}, "3": {"1": {"m": "How did the serpent enter the garden?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "How did the serpent 🐍 enter the garden"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}, "6": {"m": "This has been the problem of man ever since: we want to do the very thing we've been told not to do — and to do it without being caught — even when we've already been told exactly what will follow.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "This has been the problem of man since. We want to do what we've been asked not to do without being caught even when we've been told what would happen after."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "7": {"m": "There is a certain wisdom learned only *after* temptation. Either you succumb and learn it the hard way, by facing the consequences — or you escape the consequences by not succumbing, and behold, with wisdom, what could have happened to you had you defied it. That is what happened to Adam, and what continues to happen to us, his children.\n\nOf course, you can also gain wisdom by observation — watching what others go through and avoiding it. Though seeing what happens to others is no guarantee you'll heed it. That is how foolish and deceptive our hearts can be.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "There is a certain wisdom (lesson) that is learnt only after temptation... Of course, you can gain wisdom by observance... That's how foolish and deceptive our hearts can be."]], "c": "Light polish and paragraphing.", "e": ""}, "10": {"m": "O my Lord and my God — only God knows how that moment felt for them. A sudden, bizarre torrent of information and emotion. For the first time, they felt fear and dread. They had broken the pot, and it could not be repaired. They realised they had just attempted a failed coup — to usurp the power of the God of the universe — and failed. They saw the deception of their own ways, saw their nakedness, and hid from the Lord.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Oh, my lord and my God. Only God knows how that moment felt for them. This sudden bizerk torrents of information and emotions. For the first time, they felt fear 😱 and dread. They had broken the pot and it could not be repaired... They saw the deception of their ways and hid from the Lord."]], "c": "Polish; removed a duplicated sentence; 'bizerk'→'bizarre'.", "e": ""}, "11": {"m": "This question — \"who told you that you were naked?\" — is the key to understanding the tree. A strange question indeed.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "THIS QUESTION 🙋.. \"who told you that thou was naked\" is THE KEY 🗝 TO UNDERSTANDING THE TREE 🎄. A strange question 🙋."]], "c": "Light polish (kept your emphasis).", "e": ""}, "12": {"m": "Really — of all the excuses Adam could have reached for, he blamed his wife. To stoop so low rather than own it tells you just how terrified he was. He was dead scared, and willing to clutch at anything to win a pardon.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Really. Of all the excuses that Adam could come up with he blamed his wife. To stoop so low rather than man up... He was dead scared and was willing to couch at anything to gain pardon"]], "c": "Light polish; 'couch'→'clutch'.", "e": ""}, "21": {"m": "Such a loving God.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Such a loving God"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}, "23": {"m": "Man had rebelled and committed high treason; he could no longer enjoy the privileges of the Father. He had been living in a garden God Himself had planted — a garden he contributed nothing to, yet enjoyed the fruit of, needing only to tend and keep it. Now he is driven out to go and start his own. He had wanted to be God — that was the allure of the temptation — and now he is left on his own, to be his own god, and to see what life is like without the one true God.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Man had rebelled and committed high treason, he could no longer enjoy the privileges of the father... He wanted to be God. That was the allure of the temptation and now he was left on his own, to be his own god..."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "24": {"m": "How far did God drive them out? And the cherubim guarded specifically the way to the Tree of Life, at the east of the garden — could someone not simply have approached from the west and entered?\n\nAt first this seemed like overkill to me — cherubim and a flaming sword, just to keep Adam and his wife out. But yes: it showed and emphasised their complete rejection. They were now outcasts.\n\nThink of visiting someone when, out of nowhere, wild dogs bark at you — perhaps even a gun is pointed at you by the owner. What did you do wrong? Nothing — you simply don't belong there. Then watch how the reception changes the moment they realise you're a friend of the family: the dogs settle, the gun disappears, because you are family.\n\nSo it was with Adam. He was now an outcast, and a potentially dangerous enemy. For consider the consequence had he eaten of the tree of life and lived forever in the sin nature — most certainly, unhindered wickedness. See how much wickedness man manages in a single lifetime; now imagine it with no end. This bears more thinking.", "d": [["Oct 2023 (first thought)", "How far did God drive them out? Also, the cherubims kept specifically the way to the Tree of Life at the east of the garden.... Could not someone go through the west and entered"], ["3 Jan 2022 (update)", "This sounds like a bit of an overkill. Cherubims and a flashing sword just to keep Adam and his wife out..?? Yes. It showed and emphasized their complete rejection. They were now outcasts... We've had the experience of visiting someone and suddenly wild dogs bark... Same thing with Adam. He was now an outcast and a potentially dangerous enemy... Now imagine the wickedness man would exhibit if he lived forever. This will bear more thinking..."]], "c": "Merged your original question with your later update into one flowing reflection.", "e": "You returned to this verse and went deeper. The refined version keeps your fuller, later understanding (the 'outcast / wild dogs' insight and the danger of living forever in a sinful nature), built on your first question about why the guard seemed like 'overkill.'"}}, "4": {"1": {"m": "The same creative element of naming is unveiled again here: she calls her son Cain — a name with a specific meaning.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "The same creative element of naming is unveiled here, Calls her son Cain... A name with specific meaning"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "3": {"m": "Where is this coming from? Why does God need an offering — and when did He instruct them to begin giving one? Cain's offering was rejected. Why? Both Cain and Abel brought of the work they had done.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Where is this coming from? Why does God need an offering? When did God instruct them to start giving an offering. Cain's offering was rejected. Why? Cain brought based on the work he did as did Abel."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "4": {"m": "How did Cain and Abel know to sacrifice at all? Who initiated it? In later chapters we see Adam's descendants showing real skill — tent-making, metal-craft and more. Who taught them all this? Probably Adam, or they simply had the sense for it. Remember that Adam was created with extraordinary intelligence, and lost his place of dominion only when he sinned. From this it's clear man was not born a caveman slowly evolving into intelligence — man was created fully intelligent, knowing many things instinctively.\n\nThis is the book of beginnings, so we meet the first mention of many things. And here a question stands that we are simply not told the answer to: how did God show that He \"had respect\" to Abel's offering and not Cain's? In what visible way did He prefer the one over the other? Scripture does not say.", "d": [["Original study note", "How did Cain and Abel know to sacrifice? Who initiated it for them to know to follow?... man was not born as a cave man and started to evolve to more intelligent life form. Man was created fully intelligent and knowing many things let's say instinctively."], ["Oct 2023", "Wait oo... GOD HAD respect as in how. What did God do to show respect? This is the book of beginnings. So we see a lot of first mention of things. How did God show he preferred Abel's offering to Cains? We are not told. Why did God prefer Abel's over Crain's? We are not told"]], "c": "Merged your earlier study note with the later draft into one reflection.", "e": ""}, "5": {"m": "Why was Cain angry — especially since he knew what was right? Was he angry at God, at his brother, or both? And note: he was not merely angry, he was *very* angry. Perhaps he felt that after all the hard labour from a cursed ground that yields only through sweat and toil, his work had come to nothing — and he had lost the fruits of it for, in his eyes, no reason.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Why was Cain angry 😡 especially considering that he knew what was right? Was he angry at God or his brother or both? He was not just angry... He was very angry. Probably he felt after all the hard work from the cursed ground... his work was all to no avail. Also, he had also lost the fruits for no reason"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "8": {"m": "Why didn't God protect Abel? Despite God's warning, Cain let sin in and killed his brother — even after God told him plainly what was about to happen, that \"sin was lying at the door.\"\n\nA door? What door? Adam's generation seems remarkably advanced — and rightly so, having lived in the paradise of Eden; I wouldn't be surprised if Adam lived in a mansion there, knowing so much from his interactions with God. But back to the door: God paints a picture of sin as something *separate* from the man, crouching at the door — and Cain, wroth over nothing, lets it in.\n\nWas this not exactly what God had wanted to avoid — the very reason man was not to eat of the fruit? Cain was angry for no sound reason at all, yet he felt justified. Sin brings with it a surge of negative emotion, and to relieve that emotion we act out the way we feel, just to feel better and be rid of it.\n\nThe door also shows us something: within man is a door — to the soul, I believe — which before the fall was shut to sin, but after the fall can be opened and closed by sin, if we let it.\n\nAnd once again God is in active conversation with them, yet does nothing to interrupt this gruesome murder of an innocent man. The same was true when Adam ate the fruit — it was almost as if God did not intervene at all.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Why didn't God protect Abel? Despite God's warning Cain let sin in and killed his brother... That sin was lying at the door. DOOR?? What door? Adam and his generation seem very advanced... God paints the picture of sin being separate from the man... in man is a door, a door to the soul I believe which before the fall was shut to sin but after the fall could be opened and closed by sin if we let it. GOD was still in active conversation with them. Why does God not do anything to interrupt... Same thing when Adam was eating the fruit. It was like God didn't care."]], "c": "Polish and paragraphing; one long note organised into a flowing argument.", "e": ""}, "14": {"m": "Here is a strange thing: Cain still walked *in* the earth, yet was driven *from* it. He could walk on it and even plant in it, but the earth would no longer yield to him or support him — it no longer responded to him. He was, in the fullest sense, an outcast: even when he sowed, it would yield nothing. Which raises the question — how then was he to eat?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "note 1: He was in the earth, walked in it yet was driven away from it... note 2: He was walking in the earth yet had been driven from the face of the earth. In the sense that the earth no Longer responded to and supported him. He was like an outcast so even when he sows it will yield nothing. That strange, so how was he to eat?"]], "c": "Merged your two notes (they said the same thing twice) into one.", "e": ""}, "15": {"m": "I don't fully follow the conversation here. Why would anyone who found Cain want to kill him — was such wickedness already on the earth? It seems Cain and his line had been receiving some measure of solace, protection and favour by staying close to God through offerings; why else would Cain lament losing out by the Lord's face being hidden from him? It also seems that by now men had multiplied considerably — for someone to chance upon Cain out there. Or could it be that the sons of the angels were already on the scene?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "I don't get the conversation going on here. Why would anyone finding Cain kill him. Was such wickedness already on the earth... It seems that Cain and Co were receiving some solace, protection and favour by being close to God... Also it seems by this time men had really multiplied... Or could it mean already the sons of the angels were already here?"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "16": {"m": "How, and when? Where exactly was \"the presence of the Lord\"? It seems that although they had been driven from the garden, some form of communication still continued between them and God — and that constant fellowship in His presence brought them protection from the world outside. Cain \"went east of Eden\" — so where had he been before? Were they still lingering near the garden, hoping to get back in? Because so far it looks as though Adam's family was still together: Adam, Eve, Cain and Abel.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Ei... How and when. Where was the presence of the lord?? It seems that although they had been driven out of the garden they had some form of communication going on between them And God... Cain went east of Eden.. So where was he prior? Where they hanging around the garden with the hope of getting back..."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "5": {"6": {"m": "Here again the genealogy is traced through the younger line rather than the eldest. And land must have been wonderfully free at this very early stage.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Here again we see the genealogy given to the youngest not the oldest. Land was so free at this very early stages"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "13": {"m": "Given that the genealogy is counted through Seth rather than Cain, can we say the names listed here are not necessarily the firstborn, but rather those through whom the Messiah was traced?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Can we say based on the example of the genealogy counting from Seth instead of Cain that, the names given here are not necessarily first born but rather those through whom the messiah was traced???"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "6": {"15": {"m": "Why did God give these specific dimensions — why not smaller? Let's look ahead to verses 17-19 for the answer.", "d": [["Original study note", "Why did God give these specific dimensions? Why not smaller. Well lets see verses 17-19"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "7": {"17": {"m": "It was here that it dawned on me: the ark's specific dimensions were so it could withstand the storm that was coming. I believe there were boats and ships in that day — so when the flood rose, some surely tried to escape by canoe and the like. But the nature of that storm was such that only a vessel of the ark's calibre could survive it; and since there was only one of its kind, only Noah and his family lived. O the manifold wisdom of God.", "d": [["Original study note", "it was at this point that it dawned on me that the specific dimensions of the ark was so it could stand the storm that was to come... only a ship of the calibre of Noah's ark could survive the storm and since there was only one of such a kind, it was only NOAH and his family that survived. O the many fold wisdom of God."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "8": {"2": {"m": "So the flood was not caused by rain alone — water also gushed up from the earth and the seas to flood the mainland; a tsunami of a sort. (And I keep wondering: how was Noah able to gather every species in pairs? Could all the various species of the entire world really fit one ark? How was every kind represented?)", "d": [["Original study note", "the flood was not just caused by the rain but also water gushed up from the earth and seas to flood the main land. A tsunami of a sort. I wonder, really how was Noah able to get all of each species in pairs..."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "10": {"1": {"m": "Shem was the line through whom the Messiah was reckoned — though it isn't stated outright, I wonder who the eldest actually was.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Shem was the line through whom the messiah was reckoned...Though not emphatically stated I wonder who the eldest was."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "5": {"m": "Notice \"the isles of the Gentiles\" — yet at this point in history there was no Israel for there to *be* Gentiles. Calling them Gentiles here reflects the standpoint of the author writing later. And it makes a striking point: those Israel would later call Gentiles actually share the same father — trace the genealogy and they all go back to Noah.", "d": [["Original study note", "The people described as gentiles by Israel are actually of same father with Israel. When you trace the genealogy, they all have same fathers. Noah"], ["Oct 2023", "The isles of the Gentiles, as at this point in history there was no Israel for there to be Gentiles. They being called gentiles at this stage indicates the position of the author and the narrative as at when this was being written"]], "c": "Merged your two notes — the later one (author's standpoint) plus the earlier one (shared ancestry).", "e": ""}, "21": {"m": "The children of Eber — the name meaning \"from the other side\" — are the Hebrews. According to Dake's commentary, they came from the other side of the Euphrates.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "The children of eber (meaning from the other side are the Hebrews). They came from the other side of the Euphrates according to dakes commentary."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "32": {"m": "So from the very beginning, we were all one people.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "So from the beginning we were all one people"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}}, "11": {"2": {"m": "From which east did they journey — and was it in rebellion against God, moving away from the Eden that the pre-flood world had clung to in hope of returning to Him?\n\nAnd on “Now the Lord HAD said to Abraham” — when exactly did the Lord say it? I checked several versions; some keep “had said,” others just “said.” Either way, we are not told precisely when. But notice that Abraham’s father Terah had already set out for Canaan. Some suggest the call came first to Terah, who did not fulfil it — so it passed to Abraham. An inference, but a reasonable one.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Journeyed from which east, was it in rebellion to God, moving away from the garden of eden around which the world before hung onto in hope of returning to God\n\nNow the lord had said to Abraham...., when did the lord say unto him. Let me check other versions.. I'll be back. I'm back and several versions maintained the 'had said' and some just used 'said'. Either ways we are not told when God said to him. However we see earlier that Abraham's father had set off to go to the Land of canaan. Some say he had the calling first but did not fulfill it hence it was transferred to Abraham."]], "c": "Polish; folded your 'let me check / I'm back' aside into the finished thought.", "e": ""}}, "12": {"5": {"m": "And into the land of Canaan they came indeed. It is as if Terah had set out for Canaan but settled instead in Haran, whereas Abraham completed the very journey his father began.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "And into the land of Cannan they came indeed. It was If to say that Terah had set off to go into the land of Cannan and they ended up settling in Haran but in Abraham's case he completed the journey"]], "c": "Light polish; fixed 'Hagan'→'Haran'.", "e": ""}, "7": {"m": "Having completed his act of obedience, God now appears to give further specifics. Our walk with God is a journey: as we finish one quest of obedience, He comes with the next instruction. In between, He nudges, watches, observes, and repeats the command as needed — until we complete it, and only then do fresh orders and updates come. Until then, we simply go in circles on the same track.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Having completed his obedience, God now appears to him to give further specifics. Our walk with God is a journey. As we complete one quest of obedience, he comes with the next update... Till then we would go in circles on the same track"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "16": {"m": "This looks like fresh assets that had been acquired while in Egypt.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "This look like fresh assets that had been acquired while in Egypt"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}}, "13": {"2": {"m": "Scripture says Abraham was very rich — and doesn’t leave it vague, but goes on to specify the areas of his wealth: cattle, silver and gold.\n\nThis was the testimony coming out of Egypt — a man rich in cattle, in silver, and in God. He had sojourned in Egypt, likely leaving great wealth behind, and yet came out very rich. The same thing happens later in Isaac’s day, out of which comes so much wealth. (He may also have sold some of the menservants and donkeys he gained in Egypt, to lighten the journey back.)", "d": [["Original study note", "Abraham was very rich scripture says, but doesn't leave it blank but goes ahead and states the areas of Abrahams riches; cattle, gold and silver."], ["Oct 2023", "From Egypt, this was the testimony - a man rich in cattle and silver and God. He sojourned in Egypt, likely left his massive wealth behind and yet came out a very rich man... same thing happened in Isaac's Era... Probably Abraham sold some or all of his men-servants and asses he got in Egypt to lighten his journey back"]], "c": "Merged your two notes into one.", "e": ""}, "4": {"m": "In calling on the name of the Lord — what did Abraham actually say, and by what name did he call on Him? What were his prayer topics? For such a very rich man, what were his thoughts, his requests?\n\nSurely, among them, a seed of his own, and other generational prayers. Abraham had a need; he was broken somewhere — and it was out of that very need that God would make him an example of faith and faithfulness.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "In calling on the name of the Lord, what did Abraham say and with what name did he call on him. What were his prayer topics?... he prayed concerning a seed for himself... He Abraham had a need, he was broken somewhere and it was out of that need God was to set an example of faith and faithfulness"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "6": {"m": "They had come from Haran already wealthy, and at first their possessions still allowed them to live together. But for their property to grow so great that they could no longer dwell together — even in such vast land — needing to move days’ journey apart into different cities to thrive: then they had indeed gained massive wealth.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "In coming they were already rich from Haran but their property was such that they could live together. But for their property to be so great that they could not dwell together in such vast land. Then they indeed had gained massive wealth. They had to go days' journey away from each other."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "17": {"m": "What was the significance of this gesture — was it to survey the land? Even today, when you receive an inheritance, you survey it and walk it. But on a second reading I see God states the reason plainly: He would give it to him. It was God saying, “It is yours — feel free, walk through it.”\n\nAnd this is faith: a land full of owners and giants, and Abraham walks through it knowing it is his. What a mindset shift. He did business with them, traded and prospered above them all — yet they did not know who he was, though he knew. He knew he was the true landowner; he and God knew. Oh, the beauty of spiritual authority and revelation. In a sense, it reminds me of modern Israel.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "What was the significance of this gesture? Was it to survey the land?... On 2nd reading, God states why he should walk through the land. Because he God will give it to him... This indeed is faith. A land filled with owners and giants and Abraham walks through it knowing it is he's... He knew he was the actual landowner. He and God knew... This in a sense reminds me of modern israel."]], "c": "Polish; merged your first impression and second reading into one flow.", "e": "On first reading you took the walking as merely surveying the land; on a second reading you caught God's stated reason — that He was giving it to him. The Modern version keeps that fuller second reading."}, "18": {"m": "Abraham’s removing of his tent was a response of faith and obedience to God’s command. Anytime God spoke, Abraham acted — immediately.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Abraham removing his tent was a faith and obedience response to God's command. Anytime God spoke Abraham acted immediately"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}}, "14": {"12": {"m": "In one swoop Lot lost everything — such was the nature of those wars: a time of massive wealth transfer. Lot, who had thought himself wise in choosing the well-watered plain, was now proven foolish.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "In one swoop he lost everything. That was the nature of wars. A time of massive wealth transfer. Lot thinking he was wise was now proven foolish."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "13": {"m": "Alliances — always vital in such dangerous times of attacks and raids. And note: Abraham had his own small personal army.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Alliances, always important in such dangerous times of attacks and raids. Abraham had his own small personal army"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "14": {"m": "Abraham was remarkably generous in going to rescue his nephew Lot. Either there was no animosity between them when Lot left, or Abraham, being his uncle, was simply forgiving and generous.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Abraham was very generous in helping his nephew lot. That may well mean there was no animosity for lot when he left or Abraham being his uncle was just forgiving and generous."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "15": {"m": "I don’t quite get this — how can 300 trained men rout four kings? Perhaps the force was nearer three thousand (entirely possible), though even that doesn’t change the odds by much. Or perhaps the 300 were elite and highly skilled, like modern special forces. Most likely, though, Jehovah helped them mightily.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "I don't get this. How can 300 trained soldiers route out 4 kings? Either the soldiers were about three thousand which is entirely possible though that does still not change the odds of defeat much. It could well be the 300 where highly trained and skilled, like the US marines. Or most likely Jehovah helped them mightily."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "20": {"m": "Was this a tithe of all his wealth, or only of what he had gained through this particular conquest?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Was it tithes of all his wealth or of what he had gotten through this conquest"]], "c": "Punctuation only.", "e": ""}, "21": {"m": "Looking at the context, here Abraham paid tithes of the bounty — he was, after all, returning from battle. It makes me wonder: did Abraham pay tithes again after this? Did he tithe of all his other property too — and to whom?", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Looking at the context, here it means that Abraham paid tithes of the bounty. Obviously, he was returning from battle. I wonder, did ABRAHAM pay tithes again after this also did he pay tithes of all his other properties and to whom"]], "c": "Removed a stray reference fragment; light polish.", "e": ""}, "22": {"m": "What is the covenant name of God doing here? I had thought He was not yet known to them by the name “I AM.” This only further illustrates that the author was someone who already knew the ‘I AM.’", "d": [["Oct 2023", "What is the covenant name of God doing here? I thought by his name I am he was not known to them. That goes to further illustrate the author was one who knew the ' I am'"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "24": {"m": "So the others took their portion; it was Abraham’s own personal decision to take nothing from the king of Sodom. He detested the thought that the depraved king of a depraved city should one day brag that he had made Abram rich.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Okay so the others took their portion. It was Abraham's personal decision to not take nothing from the king of Sodom. He detested thinking that the depraved king of a depraved city would brag that he made Abram rich"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "15": {"2": {"m": "Childlessness is a deeply painful thing — and this, of all things, was to be the number-one test for the father of faith. Think about it: to qualify as the father of faith, the test was childlessness — the inability to fulfil the dominion mandate of fruitfulness.\n\n“Lord, what wilt Thou give me…” — nothing could compare to a child. Abraham would rather lose everything and have a child, and build his wealth afresh, than die without one and leave his inheritance to a servant. It is a very painful thing to die childless. Lord, help.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Childlessness is very painful. This was to be the number one test for the father of faith... the test was Childlessness. Inability to fulfil the dominion mandate of fruitfulness. Lord what will thou give me... Nothing was to be compared to a child. Abraham would rather lose everything and have a child... than to die without one and to leave his inheritance for his servant. It is a very painful thing to die childless. Lord help"]], "c": "Merged your two notes (Note 1 & Note 2) into one.", "e": ""}, "6": {"m": "Amen.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "AMEN"]], "c": "No change.", "e": ""}, "11": {"m": "Another classic example of not making assumptions when it comes to spiritual things. Abraham could have assumed that since God gave the order, he could simply lay the birds and animals out in the open and God Himself would protect them. That would have been a wrong assumption — and we believers often get hurt precisely because of the many wrong assumptions we make.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Another typical example of not making assumptions when It comes to spiritual things. He could have assumed that since God gave him the order, he could just lay the birds and animals out in the open and God would himself protect them. That would have been a wrong assumption and we often get hurt as believers because of the many wrong assumptions we make"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "18": {"m": "“Have I given this land” — not “will I give.” He had already handed it over, officially; all the other occupants were merely waiting for their lease to run out.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "'Have I given this land' not 'will I give'. He had handed it over officially, all other occupants were just waiting for their lease to run out"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}, "16": {"2": {"m": "The Lord had told Abram it would be “his seed,” so in a way Sarah’s logic holds. Yet this was still a wrong move — and interestingly, God did not interfere. He could have spared Abraham all the headache that followed.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "The Lord had spoken to Abram and said his seed, so well Sarah's logic kind of holds true. Interestingly though this was a wrong move, and God did not interphere. He could have saved Abraham all that headache subsequently."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "3": {"m": "In choosing a surrogate for her husband, Sarah naturally chose the loyal, humble one. But as life sometimes turns out, that very servant failed her and turned proud. Wisdom for us.", "d": [["Oct 2023", "In choosing a surrogate for her husband, she obviously chose the loyal and humble one. However as life sometimes turns out to be, she failed her and turned proud. Wisdom for us."]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}, "4": {"m": "Ah, people — we are just interesting. Didn’t Hagar despise Sarah because she felt used? That she must go through the pains of pregnancy, only for Sarah to lay claim over the child? She had suddenly become aware that her son was heir to one of the greatest men in the land. She would be a fool, she reasons, to simply let her baby be given to this old, barren woman. (Just thinking aloud for Hagar.)", "d": [["Oct 2023", "Women women, human beings... We are just interesting. Didn't Hagar despise Sarah because she felt used? That she has to go through the pains of pregnancy only for Sarah to lay claim over the child. She suddenly became aware that her son was heir to one of the greatest If not the greatest man in the land. She would be a fool to just let her baby be given to this old barren woman. Well just thinking aloud for Hagar"]], "c": "Light polish.", "e": ""}}}};